Suppose $P_1\,P_2$ are polyhedra, we write $P = P_1 union.sq P_2$ if $P = P_1 union P_2$ and the interiors of $P_1\,P_2$ are disjoint. Therefore, any polyhedron $P$ must have a finite decomposition into polyhedra $P = P_1 union.sq dots.h.c union.sq P_m$

we say $P$ is #strong[scissors congruent]#index-entry("scissors congruent")\(s.c.) to $Q$, denote $P tilde.op Q$, if there are decompositions $P = P_1 union.sq dots.h.c union.sq P_m$, $Q = Q_1 union.sq dots.h.c union.sq Q_m$ such that $Q_i = g_i P_i$, where $g_i in upright(I s o m)\(bb(R)^n\)$ is an isometry, we can also define more generally $G$-scissors congruence $tilde.op_G$, meaning $g_i in G lt.eq upright(I s o m)\(bb(R)^n\)$

]#label("Scissors conguence")
#statement("Remark")[
Two dimensional polytopes are called #strong[polygons]#index-entry("Polygon"), and three dimensional polytopes are called #strong[polyhedrons]#index-entry("Polyhedron")

]
#statement("Example")[

]
#statement("Theorem")[
Suppose $P\,Q$ are polygons in $bb(R)^2$, $P tilde.op Q$ iff $upright(A r e a)\(P\)= upright(A r e a)\(Q\)$

]#label("P,Q s.c. <=> P,Q have same area")
#proof(title: "Proof")[
