#strong[Hilbert's third problem.] Is there any two polyhedra of the same volume which is not scissors congruent
#strong[Answer.] YES!
#statement("Definition")[
Given a polyhedron $P$, we can define #strong[Dehn invariant]#index-entry("Dehn invariant") #math.equation(block: true, numbering: none, $D\(P\)= sum_e l\(e\)times.o frac(theta\(e\), pi) in bb(R) times.o_(bb(Z)) bb(R)\/bb(Z)$) Where $e$ runs over all the edges of $P$, $l\(e\)$ is the length of $e$, $theta\(e\)$ is the dehedral angle

]
#statement("Theorem")[
$D$ is an invariant of scissors congruence

]#label("Dehn invariant is invariant of s.c.")
#proof(title: "Proof")[

]
#statement("Lemma")[
If $b in.not bb(Q)$, then $a times.o b in bb(R) times.o_(bb(Z)) bb(R)\/bb(Z)$ is not zero

]#label("a tensor b is not zero in R tensor R mod Z if b is irrational")
#proof(title: "Proof")[
We can define a $bb(Z)$-bilinear map $chevron.l a chevron.r times chevron.l b chevron.r arrow.r bb(R)\/bb(Z)\,$ $\(n a\,m b\)mapsto\(n m b\)$, which induces a homomorphism $chevron.l a times.o b chevron.r = chevron.l a chevron.r times.o chevron.l b chevron.r arrow.r bb(R)\/bb(Z)\,$ $n m\(a times.o b\)= n a times.o m b mapsto\(n m b\)$, this is not a zero map, thus $a times.o b$ is not zero

]
#statement("Example")[
The Dehn invariant of a cube of side length $l$ is #math.equation(block: true, numbering: none, $6 l times.o 1 / 2 = 0$) The Dehn invariant of a tetrahedron of side length $l$ is #math.equation(block: true, numbering: none, $4 l times.o theta / pi$) Where $cos theta = 1 / 3$

Let's show that $theta / pi in.not bb(Q)$, suppose otherwise, then there is a positive integer $k$ such that $cos k theta = 0$, let's show $cos k theta = a_k / 3^k$, $3 divides.not a_k$ by induction, when $k = 1$, $cos theta = 1 / 3\,3 divides.not 1$ #math.equation(block: true, numbering: none, $cos\(k + 1\)theta & = 2 cos k theta cos theta - cos\(k - 1\)theta\
 & = 2 a_k / 3^k 1 / 3 - a_(k - 1) / 3^(k - 1)\
 & = frac(2 a_k - 9 a_(k - 1), 3^(k + 1))$) $3 divides 9 a_(k - 1)$ but $3 divides.not 2 a_k$

According to Lemma #ref(label("a tensor b is not zero in R tensor R mod Z if b is irrational"), supplement: none), we know the Dehn invariant of a tetrahedron isn't zero, thus a cube and a tetrahedron can never be scissors congruent due to Theorem #ref(label("Dehn invariant is invariant of s.c."), supplement: none)

]
#statement("Theorem", title: [Sydler])[
Suppose $P\,Q$ are polyhedra in $bb(R)^3$, $P tilde.op Q$ iff $upright(V o l u m e)\(P\)= upright(V o l u m e)\(Q\)$ and $D\(P\)= D\(Q\)$

]
#proof(title: "Proof")[

]
#statement("Definition")[
Suppose $X$ is a metric space with the notion of a polytope, for example $bb(R)^n$, $S^n$, $bb(H)^n$

The #strong[scissors congruence group]#index-entry("Scissors congruence group") $cal(P)\(X\)$ is defined to the free abelian group generated by polytopes $\[P\]$, modulo relations:

#strong[i:] $\[P\]=\[P_1\]+\[P_2\]$, for $P = P_1 union.sq P_2$

#strong[ii:] $\[g P\]=\[P\]$, for $g in upright(I s o m)\(bb(R)^n\)$

$upright(I s o m)\(bb(R)^n\)$ is the Isometry group of $bb(R)^n$

We can also define more generally $cal(P)\(X\,G\)$, meaning $g in G lt.eq upright(I s o m)\(bb(R)^n\)$

$P$ and $Q$ is #strong[stably scissors congruent]#index-entry("Stable scissors congruence") if $P union.sq R tilde.op Q union.sq R'$

]
#statement("Proposition")[
If $P\,Q$ are scissors congruent, $\[P\]=\[Q\]$

]
#proof(title: "Proof")[
By Definition #ref(label("Scissors conguence"), supplement: none), $P = P_1 union.sq dots.h.c union.sq P_m$, $Q = Q_1 union.sq dots.h.c union.sq Q_m$ and $Q_i = g_i P_i$, $g_i in G$, thus $\[P\]=\[P_1\]+ dots.h.c +\[P_m\]=\[Q_1\]+ dots.h.c +\[Q_m\]=\[Q\]$

]
#statement("Remark")[
We can give isometric classes of polytopes a commutative monoid stucture, then $cal(P)\(X\)$ is the Grothendieck K-group, $\[P\]=\[Q\]$ iff $P union.sq R tilde.op Q union.sq R'$, where $R' = g R$

]
#statement("Lemma")[
Suppose $P\,Q$ are generalized polytopes, and $upright(V o l u m e)\(P\)> upright(V o l u m e)\(Q\)$, then there exists a generalized polytope $R subset.eq P$ such that $Q tilde.op R$

]#label("Lemma for Zylev's theorem")
#proof(title: "Proof")[
Consider dividing into small cubes

On $S^2$, we can consider barycentric subdivision of the tetrahedron over and over again

