]
#statement("Lemma")[
$quad$ #strong[\(a)] If $X$ is a $G$ set, then $bb(Z)\[X\]$ is a $bb(Z)\[G\]$-module($G$-module)

#strong[\(b)] If $M$ is an right $R$-module, $N$ is a left $R$-module, then $M times.o_R N$ is an abelian group

]
#statement("Proposition")[
Treating $bb(Z)$ as a trivial $G$-module, i.e. $g dot.op 1 = 1$

#strong[\(a)] If $M$ is a $G$-module, $M times.o_(bb(Z)\[G\]) bb(Z) = M\/{ g m tilde.op m }$

#strong[\(b)] If $X$ is a $G$-set, $bb(Z)\[X\]times.o_(bb(Z)\[G\]) bb(Z) = bb(Z)\[X\/G\]$

#strong[\(c)] If $H ⊴ G$, $bb(Z)\[X\]times.o_(bb(Z)\[G\]) bb(Z) = bb(Z)\[X\/H\]times.o_(bb(Z)\[G\/H\]) bb(Z) = bb(Z)\[X\/G\]$

#strong[\(d)] If $H ⊴ G lt.eq upright(I s o m)\(X\)$, $cal(P)\(X\,G\)= cal(P)\(X\,H\)times.o_(bb(Z)\[G\/H\]) bb(Z)$

]#label("Conversion of the isometry group in scissors congruence")
#proof(title: "Proof")[
$quad$ #strong[\(a)] Consider $M arrow.r M times.o_(bb(Z)\[G\]) bb(Z)$, $m mapsto m times.o 1$, since $\(g m\)times.o 1 = g\(m times.o 1\)= m times.o\(g dot.op 1\)= m times.o 1$, this induce $M\/{ g m tilde.op m } arrow.r M times.o_(bb(Z)\[G\]) bb(Z)$, on the other hand, $M\/{ g m tilde.op m }$ satisfies the universal property

#strong[\(b)] $bb(Z)\[X\]\/{ g x tilde.op x } tilde.equiv bb(Z)\[X\/G\]$

#strong[\(c)] $bb(Z)\[X\/H\]\/{ macron(g) x tilde.op x } tilde.equiv bb(Z)\[X\/G\]$

#strong[\(d)] Let $S$ be the set of simplices in $X$, then $cal(P)\(X\,G\)= bb(Z)\[S\/G\]$ is a $G$-module

]
#statement("Example")[
$H = T ⊴ G = upright(I s o m)^(+)\(bb(R)^n\)$ is the translation group, $G\/H = S O\(n\)$, $cal(P)\(bb(R)^n\,S O\(n\)\)= cal(P)\(bb(R)^n\,T\)times.o_(bb(Z)\[S O\(n\)\]) bb(Z)$, $cal(P)\(X\)= cal(P)\(X\,{ 1 }\)times.o_(bb(Z)\[G\]) bb(Z)$

]
#statement("Theorem")[
$quad$ #strong[\(a)] $cal(P)\(bb(R)^1\)tilde.equiv bb(R)$

#strong[\(b)] $cal(P)\(S^1\)tilde.equiv bb(R)$

#strong[\(c)] $cal(P)\(bb(H)^1\)tilde.equiv bb(R)$

#strong[\(d)] $cal(P)\(bb(R)^2\)tilde.equiv bb(R)$

]
#proof(title: "Proof")[
$quad$ #strong[\(a)] Consider homomorphism $phi.alt : cal(P)\(bb(R)^1\)arrow.r bb(R)$, $\[I\]mapsto\|I\|$, sending an interval to its length, this is obviously surjective and injective, thus an isomorphism

#strong[\(b)] Consider homomorphism $phi.alt : cal(P)\(S^1\)arrow.r bb(R)$, $\[theta\]mapsto\|theta\|$, sending an arc(angle) to its length, this is obviously surjective and injective, thus an isomorphism

#strong[\(c)] $bb(R)^1 arrow.r^exp bb(H)^1 = { y\|y > 0 }$ is an isomorphism

#strong[\(d)] Consider homomorphism $phi.alt : cal(P)\(bb(R)^2\)arrow.r bb(R)$, $\[P\]mapsto\|P\|$, sending a polygon to its area, according to Theorem #ref(label("P,Q s.c. <=> P,Q have same area"), supplement: none), this is injective, this is clearly surjective, thus an isomorphism

]
#statement("Lemma")[
$cal(P)\(X\)$ is $2$ divisible

]#label("Scissors congruence group is two divisible")
