Denote $P = A B C$, $P_1 = A O D$, $Q_1 = A O E$, $P_2 = B O D$, $Q_2 = B O F$, $P_3 = C O E$, $Q_3 = C O F$, then we have $\[P\]=\[P_1\]+\[P_2\]+\[P_3\]+\[Q_1\]+\[Q_2\]+\[Q_3\]= 2\(\[P_1\]+\[P_2\]+\[P_3\]\)$, hence $cal(P)\(S^2\)$ is $2$ divisible, i.e. $cal(P)\(S^2\)= 2 cal(P)\(S^2\)$

]
#statement("Theorem")[
$cal(P)\(S^2\)tilde.equiv bb(R)$

]
#proof(title: "Proof")[
Consider homomorphism $phi.alt : cal(P)\(S^1\)arrow.r bb(R)$, $\[theta\]mapsto\|theta\|$, $psi : cal(P)\(S^2\)arrow.r bb(R)$, $\[P\]mapsto\|P\|$ and $Sigma : cal(P)\(S^1\)arrow.r cal(P)\(S^2\)$ defined as follow: suppose $S^1 arrow.r.hook S^2$ as equator, $N\,S$ are the north and south poles, $Sigma$ maps arc $A B subset.eq S^1$ to $A B N$ which is clearly injective

