#statement("Theorem")[
$cal(P)\(X\)= cal(P)\(X\,upright(I s o m^(+))\(X\)\)$, i.e. only orientation preserving isometries are needed

]
#proof(title: "Proof")[
By Proposition #ref(label("Conversion of the isometry group in scissors congruence"), supplement: none), $cal(P)\(X\)= cal(P)\(X\,upright(I s o m^(+))\(X\)\)times.o_(bb(Z)\[bb(Z)\/2 bb(Z)\]) bb(Z)$ since $upright(I s o m)\(X\)\/upright(I s o m^(+))\(X\)tilde.equiv bb(Z)\/2 bb(Z)$ which is generated by some reflection $r$, we just need to prove $bb(Z)\[bb(Z)\/2 bb(Z)\]$ acts trivially on $cal(P)\(X\,upright(I s o m^(+))\(X\)\)$ also due to Proposition #ref(label("Conversion of the isometry group in scissors congruence"), supplement: none)

As in Lemma #ref(label("Scissors congruence group is two divisible"), supplement: none), suppose $r_1\,r_2\,r_3$ are the reflections over $A O$, $B O$, $C O$, and there are $s_i\,i = 1\,2\,3$ such that $r_i = s_i g$, then #math.equation(block: true, numbering: none, $\[g P\] & =\[g P_1\]+\[g Q_1\]+\[g P_2\]+\[g Q_2\]+\[g P_3\]+\[g Q_3\]\
 & =\[s_1 g P_1\]+\[s_1 g Q_1\]+\[s_2 g P_2\]+\[s_2 g Q_2\]+\[s_3 g P_3\]+\[s_3 g Q_3\]\
 & =\[r_1 P_1\]+\[r_1 Q_1\]+\[r_2 P_2\]+\[r_2 Q_2\]+\[r_3 P_3\]+\[r_3 Q_3\]\
 & =\[Q_1\]+\[P_1\]+\[Q_2\]+\[P_2\]+\[Q_3\]+\[P_3\]\
 & =\[P\]$)

]
#statement("Definition")[
Suppose $X$ is a set, we can define the #strong[tuple chain complex]#index-entry("Tuple chain complex") $C_(*)\(X\)$, where $C_n\(X\)$ to be the free abelian group generated by $n + 1$ tuples $\(x_0\,dots.h.c\,x_n\)$, define the boundary map $partial : C_n\(X\)arrow.r C_(n - 1)\(X\)$, $\(x_0\,dots.h.c\,x_n\)mapsto\(- 1\)^n\(x_0\,dots.h.c\,hat(x)_i\,dots.h.c\,x_n\)$

]#label("Definition of tuple chain complex")
#statement("Lemma")[
$H_n\(C_(*)\(X\)\)= 0$ for $n > 0$, i.e. tuple chain complex is acyclic

]
#proof(title: "Proof")[
Fix $b in X$, consider $P : C_n\(X\)arrow.r C_(n + 1)\(X\)$, $\(x_0\,dots.h.c\,x_n\)mapsto\(b\,x_0\,dots.h.c\,x_n\)$, then $partial P + P partial = 1$

]
#statement("Definition")[
If $X = bb(R)^n\,S^n$ or $bb(H)^n$, we can defined the convex hull $\|\(x_0\,dots.h.c\,x_n\)\|$ to be the #strong[carrier]#index-entry("Carrier") of $\(x_0\,dots.h.c\,x_n\)$, let $C_(*)^p\(X\)$ be those tuples such that their carriers lie in a dimension $lt.eq p$ subspace, note that $C_n^k\(X\)= C_n\(X\)$ for $k gt.eq n$

]
#statement("Remark")[
Note that a $0$-dimensional hyperplane in $S^n$ are antipodal points

]
#statement("Theorem")[
Suppose $X = bb(R)^n\,bb(H)^n$, then $C_(*)\(X\)= C_(*)^n\(X\)$, we have an isomorphism $H_n\(C_(*)\(X\)\/C_(*)^(n - 1)\(X\)\)tilde.equiv cal(P)\(X\,{ 1 }\)$

]
#proof(title: "Proof")[
Define the homomorphism $phi : C_n\(X\)arrow.r cal(P)\(X\)$, $\(x_0\,dots.h.c\,x_n\)mapsto epsilon\[\|\(x_0\,dots.h.c\,x_n\)\|\]$, $epsilon = 0$ if $\|\(x_0\,dots.h.c\,x_n\)\|$ is degenerate, otherwise $epsilon = 1$ or $epsilon = - 1$ depending on if the orientation of the carrier matches with $n + 1$ tuple $\(x_0\,dots.h.c\,x_n\)$, so by definition, $phi$ is really a map $C_n\(X\)\/C_n^(n - 1)\(X\)arrow.r cal(P)\(X\)$, $C_n\(X\)\/C_n^(n - 1)\(X\)= Z_n\(C_(*)\(X\)\/C_(*)^(n - 1)\(X\)\)$ since $partial C_n\(X\)subset.eq C_(n - 1)\(X\)= C_(n - 1)^(n - 1)\(X\)$, also $phi\(B_n\(C_(*)\(X\)\/C_(*)^(n - 1)\(X\)\)\)= phi\(partial\(C_(n + 1)\(X\)\)\)= phi\(partial\(C_(n + 1)^n\(X\)\)\)= 0$, such as #math.equation(block: true, numbering: none, $phi\(partial\(x_0\,x_1\,x_2\,x_3\)\) & = phi\(\(x_1\,x_2\,x_3\)\)- phi\(\(x_0\,x_2\,x_3\)\)+ phi\(\(x_0\,x_1\,x_3\)\)- phi\(\(x_0\,x_1\,x_2\)\)\
 & =\[\|\(x_1\,x_2\,x_3\)\|\]-\[\|\(x_0\,x_2\,x_3\)\|\]+\[\|\(x_0\,x_1\,x_3\)\|\]-\[\|\(x_0\,x_1\,x_2\)\|\]\
 & =\(\[\|\(x_1\,x_2\,x_3\)\|\]+\[\|\(x_0\,x_1\,x_3\)\|\]\)-\(\[\|\(x_0\,x_1\,x_2\)\|\]-\[\|\(x_0\,x_2\,x_3\)\|\]\)\
 & =\[\|\(x_0\,x_1\,x_2\,x_3\)\|\]-\[\|\(x_0\,x_1\,x_2\,x_3\)\|\]\
 & = 0$)

