= Applications of double complex - 2/18/2020

#statement("Example")[
Define $A_(p\,q) = C_(p + q)\(V^q\)times.o_(bb(Z)\[G L\(q\,V\)\]) bb(Z)$, $V$ is a vector space, and $C_(*)$ is the tuple complex, $partial' : A_(p\,q) arrow.r A_(p - 1\,q)$ is $partial times.o 1$, $partial'' : A_(p\,q) arrow.r A_(p\,q - 1)$, $\(v_0\,dots.h.c\,v_(p + q)\)mapsto sum\(- 1\)^i\(overline(v_0)\,dots.h.c\,hat(overline(v_i))\,dots.h.c\,overline(v_(p + q))\)$ where $overline(v)$ is the projection of $v$ in $V\/chevron.l v chevron.r$

]
#statement("Theorem")[
Recall $T o r_i\(M\,N\)= H_i\(F_(*) times.o N\)$ where $F_(*)$ is a free resolution of $M$. $T o r_i\(M\,N\)= T o r_i\(N\,M\)$

]
#proof(title: "Proof")[
Let $F_(*)\,G_(*)$ be free resolutions of $M\,N$, define $C_(*\,*) = F_(*) times.o G_(*)$, then #math.equation(block: true, numbering: none, $' E_(p\,q)^1 = H_q\(F_p times.o G_(*)\)tilde.equiv F_p times.o H_q\(G_(*)\)= cases(delim: "{", F_p times.o N\, & q = 0, 0\, & upright("otherwise"))$) #math.equation(block: true, numbering: none, $'' E_(p\,q)^1 = H_p\(F_(*) times.o G_q\)tilde.equiv H_p\(F_(*)\)times.o G_q = cases(delim: "{", M times.o G_q\, & p = 0, 0\, & upright("otherwise"))$) $' E_(p\,0)^2 = T o r_p\(M\,N\)$, $'' E_(0\,q)^2 = T o r_q\(N\,M\)$, since both spectral sequences converge to $H_(*)\(T o t\)$, $T o r_i\(M\,N\)tilde.equiv g r H_i\(T o t\)tilde.equiv T o r_i\(N\,M\)$

]
#statement("Theorem", title: [Comparison theorem])[
Suppose $C_(*)\,D_(*)$ are filtered chain complexes, $f_(*) : C_(*) arrow.r D_(*)$ is a chain map preserving the filtration, i.e. $f_(*) : F_p C_(*) arrow.r F_p D_(*)$, then it induces a map on the first page $E_(p\,q)^1\(C_(*)\)= H_(p + q)\(F_p C_(*)\/F_(p - 1) C_(*)\)arrow.r H_(p + q)\(F_p D_(*)\/F_(p - 1) D_(*)\)tilde.equiv E_(p\,q)^1\(D_(*)\)$, which then induce maps on every page, including the infinity page

If the induced map $f_(*) : E^r\(C_(*)\)arrow.r E^r\(D_(*)\)$ is an isomorphism on some page, then $f : H_(*)\(C\)arrow.r H_(*)\(D\)$ is also an isomorphism

]#label("Comparison theorem")
#statement("Corollary")[
Suppose $f : C_(*\,*) arrow.r D_(*\,*)$ is a map between double complexes, and induce isomorphism $f_(*) :' E^1\(C_(*)\)arrow.r' E^1\(D_(*)\)$ or $f_(*) :'' E^1\(C_(*)\)arrow.r'' E^1\(D_(*)\)$, then $f : H_(*)\(T o t\(C_(*)\)\)arrow.r H_(*)\(T o t\(D_(*)\)\)$ is also an isomorphism

]
#statement("Definition")[
Suppose $M$ is a Riemannian manifold, a $delta$ neighborhood of $p$ is the image of $B_delta\(0\)subset.eq T_p M arrow.r^exp M$, denoted by $U_p = exp\(B_delta\(0\)\)$ where any two points in $U_p$ can be connected by a unique geodesic

]
#statement("Remark")[
A prototypical example is the sphere

]
#statement("Theorem")[
Let $C_(*)^delta\(M\)$ denote the subcomplex of the tuple complex where each tuple lie in some $delta$ neighborhood, the inclusion $C_(*)^delta\(M\)arrow.r.hook C_(*)^(upright(s i n g))\(M\)$ induces an isomorphism $H_(*)^delta\(M\)arrow.r H_(*)^(upright(s i n g))\(M\)$

]#label("delta tuple chain complex and singular chain complex induce same homology")
#proof(title: "Proof")[
Let $cal(U) = { U_i }_(i in I)$ be a covering of $M$ consists of $delta$ neighborhoods, then $U_(i_0) inter dots.h.c inter U_(i_p)$ are contractible(pick a point $x$ and connect with other points by the unique geodesics, then look at the exponential map at $x$), define double complexes

#math.equation(block: true, numbering: none, $C_(p\,q)^delta = xor.big_(\(i_0\,dots.h.c\,i_p\)) C_q^delta\(U_(i_0) inter dots.h.c inter U_(i_p)\)\,quad C_(p\,q)^(upright(s i n g)) = xor.big_(\(i_0\,dots.h.c\,i_p\)) C_q^(upright(s i n g))\(U_(i_0) inter dots.h.c inter U_(i_p)\)$) With $partial' = sum_(j = 0)^p\(- 1\)^j epsilon_(i_j)$, where $epsilon_i : C_n\(U_(i_0) inter dots.h.c inter U_(i_p)\)arrow.r.hook C_n\(U_(i_0) inter dots.h.c inter hat(U_(i_j)) inter dots.h.c inter U_(i_p)\)$ is inclusion, and $partial'' =\(- 1\)^p partial$. Then the inclusion $C_(*)^delta\(M\)arrow.r.hook C_(*)^(upright(s i n g))\(M\)$ induces a map on the double complex

It is easy to show that $xor.big C_q^delta\(U_(i_0) inter U_(i_1)\)arrow.r xor.big C_q^delta\(U_i\)arrow.r C_q^delta\(M\)arrow.r 0$ is exact, hence $H_0\(C_(*\,q)^delta\)= C_q^delta\(M\)$. Define $P_p : C_(p\,q)^delta arrow.r C_(p + 1\,q)^delta$, for any $q$ geodesic simplex $sigma$, fix $U_j$ containing $sigma$, sending $sigma$ in $C_q^delta\(U_(i_0) inter dots.h.c inter U_(i_p)\)$ to $sigma$ in $C_q^delta\(U_j inter U_(i_0) inter dots.h.c inter U_(i_p)\)$, then $partial P + P partial = 1$, hence $H_p\(C_(*\,q)^delta\)= 0\,p > 0$

Similar for $H_p\(C_(*\,q)^(upright(s i n g))\)$, then apply Theorem #ref(label("Comparison theorem"), supplement: none)

]
#statement("Example")[
We are mostly interested in tuple chain complex of $S^n$ with each tuple lie in a hemisphere, due to Theorem #ref(label("delta tuple chain complex and singular chain complex induce same homology"), supplement: none), $H_k^(pi / 2)\(S^n\)= H_k^(upright(s i n g))\(S^n\)$ is $bb(Z)$ when $k = 0\,n$ and $0$ otherwise

]

