By CW approximation theorem and cellular approximation theorem, we may assume $G times X arrow.r X$ is cellular, $X\/G$ is a CW complex, $X\/G = K\(G\,1\)$, We have free resolution $dots.h.c arrow.r C_1^(upright(c e l l))\(X\)arrow.r C_0^(upright(c e l l))\(X\)arrow.r bb(Z) arrow.r 0$, since $C_i^(upright(c e l l))\(X\/G\)tilde.equiv C_i^(upright(c e l l))\(X\)times.o_(bb(Z)\[G\]) bb(Z)$ #math.equation(block: true, numbering: none, $H_i\(K\(G\,1\)\)= H_i\(X\/G\)tilde.equiv H_i^(upright(c e l l))\(X\/G\)= H_i\(G\)$) Since $X\/G$ is connected, $H_0\(G\)= bb(Z)$

]
#statement("Remark")[
Since $G$ acts on $C_(*)\(G\)$ freely, tuple complex $C_(*)\(G\)$ on $G$ give a free resolution of $bb(Z)$, $C_k\(G\)$ consists of $\(k + 1\)$-tuples $\(g_0\,dots.h.c\,g_k\)$, denote $a_(i j) = g_i^(- 1) g_j$, then $a_(i j) = a_(j i)^(- 1)$, $a_(i j) a_(j k) = a_(i k)$ satisfies #strong[cocycle relation], $B_k\(G\)= C_k\(G\)times.o_(bb(Z)\[G\]) bb(Z)$ consists of $\[a_01\|dots.h.c\|a_(k - 1\,k)\]$, then boundary map on $B_(*)\(G\)$ would be #math.equation(block: true, numbering: none, $partial\[a_1\|dots.h.c\|a_n\]=\[a_2\|dots.h.c\|a_n\]+ sum_(k = 1)^(n - 1)\(- 1\)^k\[a_1\|dots.h.c\|a_k a_(k + 1)\|dots.h.c\|a_n\]+\(- 1\)^n\[a_1\|dots.h.c\|a_(n - 1)\]$) In particular, $partial\[a\]=\[\]-\[\]= 0$, $partial\[a_1\|a_2\]=\[a_2\]-\[a_1 a_2\]+\[a_1\]$, $partial\[a_1\|a_2\|a_3\]=\[a_2\|a_3\]-\[a_1 a_2\|a_3\]+\[a_1\|a_2 a_3\]-\[a_1\|a_2\]$

]
#statement("Example")[
#strong[\(a)] Since $K\(bb(Z)\,1\)= S^1$, $K\(bb(Z)^n\,1\)= overbrace(S^1 times dots.h.c times S^1, n) = bb(T)^n$, $H_i\(bb(Z)^n\)= H_i\(bb(T)^n\)= bb(Z)^(binom(n, i))$ by Kunneth theorem

#strong[\(b)] $K\(bb(Z)\/2 bb(Z)\,1\)= bb(R) P^oo$, $H_i\(bb(Z)\/2 bb(Z)\,1\)= H_i\(bb(R) P^oo\)= cases(delim: "{", bb(Z)\, & i = 0, bb(Z)\/2 bb(Z)\, & i > 0 upright(" even"), 0\, & i upright(" odd"))$

]
#statement("Lemma")[
$H_1\(G\)= G^(upright(a b)) = G\/\[G\,G\]$

]
#proof(title: "Proof")[

]
#statement("Definition")[
Given a group homomorphism $phi.alt : G_1 arrow.r G_2$. $phi.alt$ induce $phi.alt : K\(G_1\,1\)arrow.r K\(G_2\,1\)$ which then induce $phi.alt_(*) : H_i\(G_1\)= H_i\(K\(G_1\,1\)\)arrow.r H_i\(K\(G_2\,1\)\)= H_i\(G_2\)$. Equivalently, if $F_bullet arrow.r^epsilon bb(Z) arrow.r 0$, $F'_bullet arrow.r^epsilon bb(Z) arrow.r 0$ are free resolutions, $F'_bullet$ can be viewed as $bb(Z)\[G_1\]$ modules via $phi.alt : bb(Z)\[G_1\]arrow.r bb(Z)\[G_2\]$, i.e. $g dot.op x = phi.alt\(g\)x$, since $F_i$'s are free we can find $f_bullet : F_bullet arrow.r F'_bullet$, $f\(g x\)= g dot.op f\(x\)= phi.alt\(g\)f\(x\)$, which induce a chain map between free resolutions and then a morphism on homology. In particular, $phi.alt$ induce $B_n\(G_1\)arrow.r B_n\(G_2\)$, $\[g_1\|dots.h.c\|g_n\]mapsto\[phi.alt\(g_1\)\|dots.h.c\|phi.alt\(g_n\)\]$

]
#statement("Example")[
$C_a : G arrow.r G$, $g mapsto a g a^(- 1)$ is the conjugation, $F_bullet arrow.r^epsilon bb(Z) arrow.r 0$ is a free resolution of $bb(Z)\[G\]$ modules, $f\(x\)= a x$, $f\(g x\)= a g x = a g a^(- 1) a x = C_a\(g\)f\(x\)$ give a homomorphism $f_bullet : F_bullet arrow.r F_bullet$ which becomes the identity when tensoring with $bb(Z)\[G_1\]$ and $bb(Z)\[G_2\]$, thus $C_a$ induce identity on group homology

]
#statement("Example")[
$G = bb(Z)\/n bb(Z) = chevron.l t chevron.r$, $N = 1 + t + dots.h.c + t^(n - 1)$, then we have free resolution #math.equation(block: true, numbering: none, $dots.h.c arrow.r bb(Z)\[G\]arrow.r^N bb(Z)\[G\]arrow.r^(1 - t) bb(Z)\[G\]arrow.r^N bb(Z)\[G\]arrow.r^(1 - t) bb(Z)\[G\]arrow.r^epsilon bb(Z) arrow.r 0$) Tensor with $bb(Z)$, we get #math.equation(block: true, numbering: none, $dots.h.c arrow.r bb(Z) arrow.r^n bb(Z) arrow.r^0 bb(Z) arrow.r^n bb(Z) arrow.r^0 bb(Z) arrow.r 0$) Hence $H_i\(G\)= cases(delim: "{", bb(Z)\, & i = 0, bb(Z)\/n bb(Z)\, & i upright(" odd"), 0\, & i > 0 upright(" even"))$

]
#statement("Corollary")[
$A = bb(Z)^n times bb(Z)\/n_1 bb(Z) times dots.h.c times bb(Z)\/n_k bb(Z)$ is a finitely generated abelian group, use Kunneth formula, we can determine the group homology

]

