#statement("Definition")[
$R$ is a commutative ring, $M$ is right $R\[G\]$ module, $C_(*)\(G\)$ is the tuple complex, equivalently, $B_(*)\(G\)$ is the bar complex, $macron(B)_(*)\(G\)= B_(*)\(G\)times.o_(R\[G\]) R$, thus #math.equation(block: true, numbering: none, $M times.o_(R\[G\]) B_(*)\(G\)tilde.equiv M times.o_R R times.o_(R\[G\]) B_(*)\(G\)tilde.equiv M times.o_R macron(B)_(*)\(G\)$) Group homology with coefficients in $M$ is #math.equation(block: true, numbering: none, $H_k\(G\;M\) & = H_k\(M times.o_(R\[G\]) C_(*)\(G\)\)\
 & = H_k\(M times.o_(R\[G\]) B_(*)\(G\)\)\
 & = H_k\(M times.o_R macron(B)_(*)\(G\)\)\
 & = T o r_k^(R\[G\])\(M\,R\)$) The differential of $M times.o_(bb(Z)\[G\]) C_(*)\(G\)$ is given by #math.equation(block: true, numbering: none, $partial\(m times.o\[g_1\|dots.h.c\|g_n\]\) & = partial\(m times.o\(1\,g_1\,g_1 g_2\,dots.h.c\,g_1 dots.h.c g_n\)\)\
 & = m g_1 times.o\[g_2\|dots.h.c\|g_n\]+ sum_(i = 1)^(n - 1)\(- 1\)^i m times.o\[g_1\|dots.h.c\|g_i g_(i + 1)\|dots.h.c\|g_n\]\
 & quad +\(- 1\)^n m times.o\[g_1\|dots.h.c\|g_(n - 1)\]$) $H_0\(G\;M\)= M times.o_(R\[G\]) R = M_G$. Write $H_k\(G\)$ for $H_k\(G\;bb(Z)\)$

]
#statement("Remark")[
A right $R\[G\]$ module $M$ can be viewed as a left $R\[G\]$ module and vice versa via $g^(- 1) m = m g$. Therefore if $M$ is a left $bb(Z) G$ module, then #math.equation(block: true, numbering: none, $partial\(m times.o\[g_1\|dots.h.c\|g_n\]\)= thin thin & g_1^(- 1) m times.o\[g_2\|dots.h.c\|g_n\]+ sum_(i = 1)^(n - 1)\(- 1\)^i m times.o\[g_1\|dots.h.c\|g_i g_(i + 1)\|dots.h.c\|g_n\]\
 & +\(- 1\)^n m times.o\[g_1\|dots.h.c\|g_(n - 1)\]$)

]
#statement("Definition")[
$cal(A)$ is an abelian category with enough projectives, a #strong[Cartan-Eilenberg resolution]#index-entry("Cartan-Eilenberg resolution") $P_(* *)$ of chain complex $A_(*)$ is an upper half plane double complex consist of projectives and a augmentation $P_(* 0) arrow.r^epsilon A_(*)$ such that

+ If $A_p = 0$, then $P_(p *) = 0$

+ $B_p^h\(P\)arrow.r^(B_p\(epsilon\)) B_p\(A_(*)\)$, $H_p^h\(P\)arrow.r^(H_p\(epsilon\)) H_p\(A_(*)\)$ are projective resolutions

]
#statement("Lemma")[
Every chain complex $A_(*)$ has a Cartan-Eilenberg resolution, and $Z_p^h\(P\)arrow.r^(Z_p\(epsilon\)) Z_p\(A_(*)\)$, $P_(p *) arrow.r^(epsilon_p) A_p$ are projective resolutions

]
#statement("Definition")[
$f\,g : D arrow.r E$ are maps between double complexes, a chain homotopy from $f$ to $g$ consists of $s^h : D_(p q) arrow.r E_(p + 1\,q)$ and $s^v : D_(p q) arrow.r E_(p\,q + 1)$ satisfying #math.equation(block: true, numbering: none, $f - g =\(s^h d^h + d^h s^h\)=\(s^v d^v + d^v s^v\)$) #math.equation(block: true, numbering: none, $s^v d^h + d^h s^v = s^h d^v + d^v s^h = 0$) So that $s^h + s^v : T o t\(D\)_n arrow.r T o t\(E\)_(n + 1)$ is a chain homotopy between $T o t\(f\)\,T o t\(g\): T o t^xor\(D\)arrow.r T o t^xor\(E\)$

]
#statement("Lemma")[
$f\,g : A_(*) arrow.r B_(*)$ are chain homotopic, $P arrow.r A_(*)$, $Q arrow.r B_(*)$ are Cartan-Eilenberg resolutions, $tilde(f)\,tilde(g) : P arrow.r Q$ are over $f\,g$, then $tilde(f)\,tilde(g)$ are chain homotopic

Any two Cartan-Eilenberg resolutions of $P arrow.r A_(*)$, $Q arrow.r A_(*)$ are chain homotopic. If $F$ is an additive functor, then $T o t^xor\(F\(P\)\)$, $T o t^xor\(F\(Q\)\)$ are chain homotopic

]#label("Chain homotopy between Cartna-Eilenberg resolutions")
#statement("Definition")[
$cal(A)\,cal(B)$ are abelian categories, $cal(A)$ has enough projectives, $F : cal(A) arrow.r cal(B)$ is an additive functor, $f : A_(*) arrow.r B_(*)$ is a map of chain complexes. The #strong[left hyper-derived functor] of $F$ is $bb(L)_i F : upright(bold(C h)) cal(A) arrow.r cal(B)$ given by $bb(L)_i F\(A_(*)\)= H_i\(T o t^xor\(F\(P\)\)\)$ which we just write as $H_i\(F\(P\)\)$, is independent of the choice of Cartan-Eilenberg resolution $P$ thanks to Lemma #ref(label("Chain homotopy between Cartna-Eilenberg resolutions"), supplement: none)

]
#statement("Definition")[
$A_(*)$ is a chain of $R G$ modules. Since $A_(*) times.o_(R\[G\]) B_(*)\(G\)= A_(*) times.o_R macron(B)_(*)\(G\)$ is a Cartan-Eilenberg resolution of $F\(A_(*)\)$ with $F = - times.o_(bb(Z)\[G\]) bb(Z)$. Hence we define the #strong[hyperhomology] of a chain complex of $R G$ modules $A_(*)$ to be #math.equation(block: true, numbering: none, $bb(H)_i\(G\,A_(*)\)= bb(L)_i F\(A_(*)\)= H_i\(A_(*) times.o_(bb(Z) G) B_(*)\(G\)\)$)

]
#statement("Lemma")[
$A_(*)$ is an acyclic chain complex with $H_0\(A_(*)\)= M$, then $bb(L)_i F\(A_(*)\)= L_i F\(M\)$. In particular, $bb(H)_i\(G\;A_(*)\)tilde.equiv H_i\(G\;M\)$

]#label("Hyperhomology of an acyclic chain complex is the same as homology")
#proof(title: "Proof")[
By Lemma #ref(label("Chain homotopy between Cartna-Eilenberg resolutions"), supplement: none), it suffices to consider the case where $A_(*)$ is the chain complex with only one nonzero term $M$ at degree zero. Suppose $P arrow.r M$ is a projective resolution of $M$, it can be regared as a Cartan-Eilenberg resolution of $A_(*)$, thus $bb(L)_i F\(A_(*)\)= H_i\(F\(P\)\)= L_i F\(M\)$

]
#statement("Theorem", title: [Hyperhomology spectral sequence])[
#index-entry("Hyperhomology spectral sequence") $L_p F\(H_q\(A_(*)\)\)arrow.r.double bb(L)_(p + q) F\(A_(*)\)$. If $A_(*)$ is bounded below, then $H_p\(L_q F\(A_(*)\)\)arrow.r.double bb(L)_(p + q) F\(A_(*)\)$

]#label("Hyperhomology spectral sequence")
#proof(title: "Proof")[
Consider the double complex $P$ of a Cartan-Eilenberg resolution $P arrow.r A_(*)$. Since $H_p^h\(P\)arrow.r H_p A_(*)$, $P_(p *) arrow.r A_p$ are projective resolutions, we have #math.equation(block: true, numbering: none, $L_p F\(H_q\(A_(*)\)\)= H_p^v F\(H_q^h\(P\)\)= H_p^v\(H_q^h F\(P\)\)='' E_(p q)^2 arrow.r.double H_(p + q) F\(P\)= bb(L)_(p + q) F\(A_(*)\)$) If $A_(*)$ is bounded below, then $' E_(p q)^1 = L_q F\(A_p\)= H_q^v\(F\(P_(p *)\)\)$ and #math.equation(block: true, numbering: none, $H_p\(L_q F\(A_(*)\)\)= H_p^h H_q^v\(F\(P\)\)=' E_(p q)^2 arrow.r.double H_(p + q) F\(P\)= bb(L)_(p + q) F\(A_(*)\)$)

]
#statement("Theorem", title: [Grothendieck spectral sequence])[
#index-entry("Grothendieck spectral sequence") $cal(A)\,cal(B)$ have enough projectives, $F : cal(B) arrow.r cal(C)$, $G : cal(A) arrow.r cal(B)$ are right exact functors and $G$ sends projectives to $F$-acyclic objects, then #math.equation(block: true, numbering: none, $\(L_p F\)\(L_q G\)\(A\)arrow.r.double L_(p + q)\(F G\)\(A\)$)

]#label("Grothendieck spectral sequence")
#proof(title: "Proof")[
Suppose $P arrow.r A$ is a projective resolution, then by Theorem #ref(label("Hyperhomology spectral sequence"), supplement: none), we have #math.equation(block: true, numbering: none, $\(L_p F\)\(L_q G\)\(A\)tilde.equiv L_p F\(H_q G\(P\)\)arrow.r.double bb(L)_(p + q)\(F G\)\(A\)$) #math.equation(block: true, numbering: none, $H_p\(L_q F\(G\(P\)\)\)arrow.r.double bb(L)_(p + q)\(F G\)\(A\)$) Since $G\(A\)$ is $F$-acyclic, $' E_2^(p q) = 0$ for $q eq.not 0$ and #math.equation(block: true, numbering: none, $E_2^(p 0) = H_p\(F G\(P\)\)= L_p\(F G\)\(A\)tilde.equiv bb(L)_p\(F G\)\(A\)$)

]
#statement("Corollary", title: [Hochschild-Serre spectral sequence])[
#index-entry("Hochschild-Serre spectral sequence") $N ⊴ G$ is a normal subgroup, $A$ is a $bb(Z) G$ module, then #math.equation(block: true, numbering: none, $H_p\(G\/N\;H_q\(N\;A\)\)arrow.r.double H_(p + q)\(G\;A\)$)

]#label("Hochschild-Serre spectral sequence")
#proof(title: "Proof")[
Consider right exact functors #math.equation(block: true, numbering: none, $F = - times.o_(bb(Z)\[G\/N\]) bb(Z) : bb(Z)\[G\/N\]upright("-mod") arrow.r bb(Z) upright("-mod")$) #math.equation(block: true, numbering: none, $G = - times.o_(bb(Z)\[N\]) bb(Z) = - times.o_(bb(Z)\[G\]) bb(Z)\[G\/N\]: bb(Z)\[G\]upright("-mod") arrow.r bb(Z)\[G\/N\]upright("-mod")$) The left derived functors of $F G = - times.o_(bb(Z)\[G\]) bb(Z)$ is $L_(*)\(F G\)\(A\)= T o r_(*)^(bb(Z)\[G\])\(A\,bb(Z)\)= H_(*)\(G\;A\)$. For any $bb(Z)\[G\]$ module $A$ and $bb(Z)\[G\/N\]$ module $B$, we have natural isomorphism #math.equation(block: true, numbering: none, $H o m_(bb(Z)\[G\/N\])\(A times.o_(bb(Z)\[G\]) bb(Z)\[G\/N\]\,B\)tilde.equiv H o m_(bb(Z)\[G\])\(A\,B\)= H o m_(bb(Z)\[G\])\(A\,U\(B\)\)$) Hence $G$ is left adjoint to forgetful functor $U$ which is exact, this implies that $G$ preserves projectives which are exactly $F$-acyclic objects. Apply Theorem #ref(label("Grothendieck spectral sequence"), supplement: none) we have #math.equation(block: true, numbering: none, $H_p\(G\/N\;H_q\(N\;A\)\)=\(L_p F\)\(L_q G\)\(A\)arrow.r.double L_(p + q)\(F G\)\(A\)= H_(p + q)\(G\;A\)$)

]
#statement("Lemma", title: [Center kills lemma])[
#index-entry("Center kills lema") $M$ is a right $R\[G\]$ module, $gamma in Z\(G\)$ such that $x gamma = r x\,forall x in M$ for some $r in R$, then $\(r - 1\)$ annihilates $H_(*)\(G\;M\)$

]#label("Center kills lemma")
#proof(title: "Proof")[
Any $gamma in G$ induce endomorphism $M times.o_R macron(B)_(*)\(G\)$ #math.equation(block: true, numbering: none, $gamma_(*)\(x times.o\[g_1\|dots.h.c\|g_q\]\)= x gamma times.o\[gamma g_1 gamma^(- 1)\|dots.h.c\|gamma g_q gamma^(- 1)\]$) Which is chain homotopic to identity through #math.equation(block: true, numbering: none, $s\(x times.o\[g_1\|dots.h.c\|g_q\]\)= sum_(i = 0)^q x times.o\[g_1\|dots.h.c\|g_i\|gamma\|gamma g_(i + 1) gamma^(- 1)\|dots.h.c\|gamma g_1 gamma^(- 1)\]$) If $gamma in Z\(G\)$ such that $x gamma = r x\,forall x in M$ for some $r in R$, then $gamma_(*)\(x times.o\[g_1\|dots.h.c\|g_q\]\)= r x times.o\[g_1\|dots.h.c\|g_q\]$ which equals $x times.o\[g_1\|dots.h.c\|g_q\]$ in $H_(*)\(G\;M\)$

]
#statement("Lemma", title: [Shapiro's lemma])[
#index-entry("Shapiro’s lemma") $H lt.eq G$, $M$ is a left $R\[G\]$ module, then there is a natural isomorphism $H_(*)\(H\;M\)tilde.equiv H_(*)\(G\;M times.o_(R\[H\]) R\[G\]\)$

]#label("Shapiro's lemma")
#proof(title: "Proof")[
$B_(*)\(H\)\,B_(*)\(G\)$ are both free resolutions of $R\[H\]$ modules of $R$, thus inclusion $M times.o_(R H) B_(*)\(H\)arrow.r.hook M times.o_(R H) B_(*)\(G\)$ induces an isomorphism on homology #math.equation(block: true, numbering: none, $H_k\(G\;M times.o_(R\[H\]) R\[G\]\) & = H_k\(M times.o_(R\[H\]) R\[G\]times.o_(R\[G\]) B_(*)\(G\)\)\
 & tilde.equiv H_k\(M times.o_(R\[H\]) B_(*)\(G\)\)\
 & tilde.equiv T o r_k^(R\[H\])\(M\,R\)\
 & tilde.equiv H_k\(M times.o_(R\[H\]) B_(*)\(H\)\)\
 & tilde.equiv H_k\(H\;M\)$)

]
