#statement("Definition")[
On $F P^1 = F union { oo }$ the group of #strong[Möbius transformations] $f\(z\)= frac(a z + b, c z + d)$ can be identified with the group of projective transformations $P G L\(2\,F\)= P S L\(2\,F\)$ since denoting $z = z_1 / z_2$ we have #math.equation(block: true, numbering: none, $[z \, 1] =\[z_1\,z_2\] & mapsto mat(delim: "(", a, b; c, d) dot.op\[z_1\,z_2\]\
 & =\[a z_1 + b z_2\,c z_1 + d z_2\]\
 & = [frac(a z_1 + b z_2, c z_1 + d z_2) \, 1]\
 & = [frac(a z + b, c z + d) \, 1]$) The #strong[Cross ratio]#index-entry("Cross ratio") of $z_0\,z_1\,z_2\,z_3 in F P^1$ is #math.equation(block: true, numbering: none, $\(z_0\,z_1\;z_2\,z_3\)= frac(\(z_2 - z_0\)\(z_3 - z_1\), \(z_3 - z_0\)\(z_2 - z_1\))$)

]
#statement("Lemma")[
+ Cross ratio is a projective invariant

+ There is a unique $g in P S G L\(2\,F\)= P S L\(2\,F\)$ such that $\(z_0\,z_1\,z_2\,z_3\)= g\(oo\,0\,1\,\(z_0\,z_1\;z_2\,z_3\)\)$

]
#proof(title: "Proof")[
+ Since #math.equation(block: true, numbering: none, $frac(a z + b, c z + d) - frac(a w + b, c w + d) = frac(\(a d - b c\)\(z - w\), \(c z + d\)\(c w + d\))$) We have #math.equation(block: true, numbering: none, $(frac(a z_0 + b, c z_0 + d) \, frac(a z_1 + b, c z_1 + d) \; frac(a z_2 + b, c z_2 + d) \, frac(a z_3 + b, c z_3 + d)) & = frac(\(a d - b c\)^4\(z_2 - z_0\)\(z_3 - z_1\), \(c z_0 + d\)\(c z_1 + d\)\(c z_2 + d\)\(c z_3 + d\)) / frac(\(a d - b c\)^4\(z_3 - z_0\)\(z_2 - z_1\), \(c z_0 + d\)\(c z_1 + d\)\(c z_2 + d\)\(c z_3 + d\))\
   & = frac(\(z_2 - z_0\)\(z_3 - z_1\), \(z_3 - z_0\)\(z_2 - z_1\))\
   & =\(z_0\,z_1\;z_2\,z_3\)$)

+ $g = mat(delim: "(", a_2 - a_0, a_1\(a_0 - a_2\); a_2 - a_1, a_0\(a_1 - a_2\))$

]
#statement("Definition")[
The #strong[ideal points] $partial bb(H)^n$ of $bb(H)^n$ in the Klein and Poincaré disc models are the boundary circles, in the half space model is $bb(R)^n union { oo }$, $overline(bb(H)^n) = bb(H)^n union partial bb(H)^n$

$cal(P)\(partial bb(H)^n\)$ is the $K_0$-group generated by simplices in $overline(bb(H)^n)$ with vertices on $partial bb(H)^n$. Explicitly, $cal(P)\(partial bb(H)^n\)= H_0\(G\;H_n\(C_(*)\(X\)\/F_(n - 1) C_(*)\(X\)\)^t\)$ are $\(n + 1\)$-tuples modulo relations $\(a_0\,dots.h.c\,a_n\)= 0$ if lies in a subspace, $\(g a_0\,dots.h.c\,g a_n\)=\(det g\)\(a_0\,dots.h.c\,a_n\)$, and $sum_(i = 0)^(n + 1)\(a_0\,dots.h.c\,hat(a_i)\,dots.h.c\,a_(n + 1)\)= 0$ for $a_i in partial bb(H)^n$

]
#statement("Theorem")[
$bb(H)^n arrow.r.hook overline(bb(H)^n)$ induce an isomorphism $cal(P)\(bb(H)^n\)arrow.r cal(P)\(overline(bb(H)^n)\)$

#math.equation(block: true, numbering: none, $H_1\(O\(n\)\,S t\(S^(n - 1)\)\)arrow.r cal(P)\(partial bb(H)^n\)arrow.r cal(P)\(overline(bb(H)^n)\)arrow.r H_0\(O\(n\)\,S t\(S^(n - 1)\)\)arrow.r 0$) Where $S t\(S^(n - 1)\)= H_n\(C_(*)\(S^(n - 1)\)\/F_(n - 1) C_(*)\(S^(n - 1)\)\)$ with tuples contained in hemispheres

If $n$ is odd, then $cal(P)\(partial bb(H)^n\)arrow.r cal(P)\(overline(bb(H)^n)\)$ is surjective with kernel consists of elements of at most order $2$

If $n > 0$ is even, then $cal(P)\(overline(bb(H)^n)\)\/upright(i m\(cal(P)\(partial bb(H)^n\)arrow.r cal(P)\(overline(bb(H)^n)\)\)) tilde.equiv cal(P)\(S^(n - 1)\)\/Sigma cal(P)\(S^(n - 2)\)$

]
#proof(title: "Proof")[

]
#statement("Example")[
$partial bb(H)^3 = bb(R)^2 union { oo } = bb(C) union { oo }$ is the Riemann sphere, every isometry on $bb(H)^3$ restricts to a conformal map on $partial bb(H)^3$ since isometry sends hemispheres to hemispheres or orthogonal planes. Isometries on $partial bb(H)^3$ are möbius transformations, and translations $z mapsto z + lambda$ can be extended to $\(z\,x_3\)mapsto\(z + lambda\,x_3\)$, dilations $z mapsto lambda z$ can be extended to $\(z\,x_3\)mapsto\(lambda z\,\|lambda\|x_3\)$, inversions $z mapsto - 1 / z$ can be extended to $\(z\,x_3\)mapsto (frac(- macron(z), \|z\|^2 + x_3^2) \, frac(x_3, \|z\|^2 + x_3^2))$. Thus the isometry group for $bb(H)^3$ is $P S L\(2\,bb(C)\)times.l bb(Z)\/2 bb(Z)$

]
#statement("Definition")[
#strong[Polylogarithm]#index-entry("Polylogarithm") is #math.equation(block: true, numbering: none, $upright(L i)_s\(z\)= sum_(k = 1)^oo z^k / k^s$) In particular, $upright(L i)_1\(z\)= - ln\(1 - z\)$, $upright(L i)_2\(z\)$ is #strong[dilogarithm]#index-entry("Dilogarithm") with analytic continuation #math.equation(block: true, numbering: none, $upright(L i)_2\(z\)= - integral_0^z frac(ln\(1 - u\), u) d u$) integrated avoiding the the cut $\[1\,oo\]$

The #strong[Bloch-Wigner function]#index-entry("Bloch-Wigner function") is #math.equation(block: true, numbering: none, $D_2\(z\)= upright(I m)\(upright(L i)_2\(z\)\)+ arg\(1 - z\)ln\|z\|$) For $z in bb(C)\\{ 0\,1 }$

]
#statement("Lemma")[
The signed volume of the ideal simplex $\(p_0\,p_1\,p_2\,p_3\)$ is $D_2\(p_0\,p_1\;p_2\,p_3\)$

]
#statement("Definition")[
$F$ is a field, define $cal(P)_F$ to be the abelian group generated by $z in F\\{ 0\,1 }$ modulo relation #numbered-equation($z_1 - z_2 + z_2 / z_1 - frac(1 - z_2, 1 - z_1) + frac(1 - z_2^(- 1), 1 - z_1^(- 1)) = 0$)#label("Relation for PF") For $z_1 eq.not z_2$

]#label("Definition for P_F")
#statement("Lemma")[
If $overline(F) = F$, then $z + z^(- 1) = 0$, $z + { 1 - z } = 0$

]
#statement("Remark")[
By adding $0 = 1 = oo = 0$, these relations are true for all $z in F union { oo }$

]
#proof(title: "Proof")[
Replace $z_j$ with $z_j^(- 1)$ in #ref(label("Relation for PF"), supplement: none) we have #numbered-equation($z_1^(- 1) - z_2^(- 1) + z_1 / z_2 - frac(1 - z_2^(- 1), 1 - z_1^(- 1)) + frac(1 - z_2, 1 - z_1) = 0$)#label("Relation for PF - eq1") Sum #ref(label("Relation for PF"), supplement: none) and #ref(label("Relation for PF - eq1"), supplement: none) we get #numbered-equation($z_1 - z_2 + z_1^(- 1) - z_2^(- 1) + z_2 / z_1 + z_1 / z_2 = 0$)#label("Relation for PF - eq2") Switch $z_1\,z_2$ we have #numbered-equation($z_2 - z_1 + z_2^(- 1) - z_1^(- 1) + z_1 / z_2 + z_2 / z_1 = 0$)#label("Relation for PF - eq3") Denote $z = z_2 / z_1$ and sum #ref(label("Relation for PF - eq2"), supplement: none) and #ref(label("Relation for PF - eq3"), supplement: none) we get #math.equation(block: true, numbering: none, $2\(z + z^(- 1)\)= 0$) Replace $z_j$ with $z^j$ in #ref(label("Relation for PF - eq2"), supplement: none) we have #math.equation(block: true, numbering: none, $z^2 + z^(- 2) = 2\(z + z^(- 1)\)= 0$) Since $overline(F) = F$, $z + z^(- 1) = 0$ in $cal(P)_F$

Replace $z_j$ with $1 - z_j$ in #ref(label("Relation for PF"), supplement: none) we have #numbered-equation(${ 1 - z_1 } - { 1 - z_2 } + frac(1 - z_2, 1 - z_1) - z_2 / z_1 + frac(1 - z_1^(- 1), 1 - z_2^(- 1))$)#label("Relation for PF - eq4") Sum #ref(label("Relation for PF"), supplement: none) and #ref(label("Relation for PF - eq2"), supplement: none) we get #math.equation(block: true, numbering: none, $ & z_1 + { 1 - z_1 } - z_2 - { 1 - z_2 } + frac(1 - z_2^(- 1), 1 - z_1^(- 1)) + frac(1 - z_1^(- 1), 1 - z_2^(- 1)) = 0\
 & arrow.r.double z_1 + { 1 - z_1 } = z_2 + { 1 - z_2 }$) Since $overline(F) = F$, $z^2 - z + 1 = 0$ has roots, then $1 - z = z^(- 1)$, thus $z + { 1 - z } = 0$ in $cal(P)_F$

]
#statement("Theorem", title: [K-groups of fields])[
$F$ is a field. The $K_0\,K_1\,K_2$ groups of $F$ are #math.equation(block: true, numbering: none, $K_0\(F\) & = bb(Z)\,thin K_1\(F\)= F^times\
K_2\(F\) & = F^times times.o_(bb(Z)) F^times\/chevron.l a times.o\(1 - a\)chevron.r\,thin a eq.not 0\,1$)

]
#statement("Theorem", title: [Bloch-Wigner])[
$F$ is algebraically closed field with characteristic $0$, write $G = S L\(2\,F\)$, we have an exact sequence #math.equation(block: true, numbering: none, $0 arrow.r bb(Q)\/bb(Z) arrow.r H_3\(G\;bb(Z)\)arrow.r^sigma cal(P)_F arrow.r^lambda and.big_(bb(Z))^2\(F^times\/mu_F\)arrow.r^("sym") H_2\(G\;bb(Z)\)arrow.r 0$) $bb(Q)\/bb(Z) tilde.equiv H_3\(mu_F\;bb(Z)\)arrow.r H_3\(G\;bb(Z)\)$ is induced by #math.equation(block: true, numbering: none, $mu_F arrow.r G\,thin z mapsto mat(delim: "(", z, 0; 0, z^(- 1))$) For any $\(g_0\,g_1\,g_2\,g_3\)in B_3\(G\)$ #math.equation(block: true, numbering: none, $sigma\(g_0\,g_1\,g_2\,g_3\)=\(g_0 oo\,g_1 oo\,g_2 oo\,g_3 oo\)$) Or for any $\[g_1\|g_2\|g_3\]in macron(B)_3\(G\)$ #math.equation(block: true, numbering: none, $sigma\(\[g_1\|g_2\|g_3\]\)= sigma\(1\,g_1\,g_1 g_2\,g_1 g_2 g_3\)=\(oo : g_1 oo : g_1 g_2 oo : g_1 g_2 g_3 oo\)$) This doesn't depend on the choice of $oo$ #math.equation(block: true, numbering: none, $lambda\(z\)= z and\(1 - z\)$) #math.equation(block: true, numbering: none, $"sym"\(u and v\)= u times.o v$) $K_2\(F\)tilde.equiv H_2\(G\;bb(Z)\)$

]
#proof(title: "Proof")[
Let $C_(*)$ be the tuple complex of $F P^1$. The stabilizer of $oo =\[1\,0\]$ is the Borel subgroup #math.equation(block: true, numbering: none, $B = {mat(delim: "(", a, b; 0, a^(- 1)) mid(bar.v) a in F^times \, b in F}$) Hence $F P^1 tilde.equiv G\/B$. The stabilizer of $oo$ and $0 =\[0\,1\]$ is the split torus #math.equation(block: true, numbering: none, $T = {mat(delim: "(", a, 0; 0, a^(- 1)) mid(bar.v) a in F^times} tilde.equiv F^times$) Hence $F P^1 times F P^1 tilde.equiv G\/T$. The stabilizer of $oo\,0$ and $1 =\[1\,1\]$ is #math.equation(block: true, numbering: none, ${mat(delim: "(", plus.minus 1, 0; 0, plus.minus 1)} tilde.equiv bb(Z)\/2 bb(Z)$) Hence $F P^1 times F P^1 times F P^1 tilde.equiv G$. Therefore we have #math.equation(block: true, numbering: none, $C_0 & = bb(Z)\[G\/B\]= bb(Z) G times.o_(bb(Z) B) bb(Z)\
C_1 & = bb(Z)\[G\/T\]= bb(Z) G times.o_(bb(Z) T) bb(Z)\
C_2 & = bb(Z)\[G\/\(bb(Z)\/2 bb(Z)\)\]= bb(Z) G times.o_(bb(Z)\[bb(Z)\/2 bb(Z)\]) bb(Z)$) By Shapiro's lemma #ref(label("Shapiro's lemma"), supplement: none), we get #math.equation(block: true, numbering: none, $H_(*)\(G\;C_0\) & = H_(*)\(G\;bb(Z) G times.o_(bb(Z) B) bb(Z)\)= H_(*)\(B\;bb(Z)\)\
H_(*)\(G\;C_1\) & = H_(*)\(G\;bb(Z) G times.o_(bb(Z) T) bb(Z)\)= H_(*)\(T\;bb(Z)\)\
H_(*)\(G\;C_2\) & = H_(*)\(G\;bb(Z) G times.o_(bb(Z)\[bb(Z)\/2 bb(Z)\]) bb(Z)\)= H_(*)\(bb(Z)\/2 bb(Z)\;bb(Z)\)= cases(delim: "{", bb(Z) & * = 0, bb(Z)\/2 bb(Z) & * upright(" odd"), 0 & * > 0 upright(" even"))$) #math.equation(block: true, numbering: none, $C_0 times.o_(bb(Z) T) B_1\(T\)arrow.r.hook C_0 times.o_(bb(Z) T) B_1\(G\)arrow.r.hook C_0 times.o_(bb(Z) G) B_1\(G\)$) induce isomorphisms on homology Consider split exact sequence

